{"name":"Forest Fruit Pink","hex":"#68393b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"forest","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A dense uncultivated tract of trees and undergrowth, larger than woods.","Any dense collection or amount."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover an area with trees."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/forest"},{"term":"fruit","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A product of fertilization in a plant, specifically:\n\nThe seed-bearing part of a plant; often edible, colourful, fragrant, and sweet or sour; produced from a floral ovary after fertilization.\nThe spores of cryptogams and their accessory organs.","The seed-bearing part of a plant; often edible, colourful, fragrant, and sweet or sour; produced from a floral ovary after fertilization."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To produce fruit, seeds, or spores."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fruit"},{"term":"pink","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A color reminiscent of pinks, the flowers.","Magenta, the colour evoked by red and blue light when combined."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a colour between red and white; pale red.","Of a fox-hunter's jacket: scarlet."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pink"}],"entries":[{"title":"Non-timber forest product","blocks":[{"text":"Non-timber forest products (NTFPs) are useful foods, substances, materials and/or commodities obtained from forests other than timber. Harvest ranges from wild collection to farming. They typically include game animals, fur-bearers, nuts, seeds, berries, mushrooms, oils, sap, foliage, pollarding, medicinal plants, peat, mast, fuelwood, fish, insects, spices, and forage. Overlapping concepts include non-wood forest products (NWFPs), wild forest products, minor forest produce, special, minor, alternative and secondary forest products – for further distinctions see the definition section below"}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Mahua.jpg/330px-Mahua.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Non-timber_forest_product"},{"title":"Forest","blocks":[{"text":"A forest is an ecosystem characterized by a dense community of trees. Hundreds of definitions of forest are used throughout the world, incorporating factors such as tree density, tree height, land use, legal standing, and ecological function. The United Nations' Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO) defines a forest as, \"Land spanning more than 0.5 hectares with trees higher than 5 meters and a canopy cover of more than 10 percent, or trees able to reach these thresholds in situ. It does not include land that is predominantly under agricultural or urban use.\" Using this definition, Global Forest Resources Assessment 2025 found that forests covered 4.14 billion hectares, or approximately 31 percent of the world's land area in 2025."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Aerial_view_of_the_Amazon_Rainforest.jpg/330px-Aerial_view_of_the_Amazon_Rainforest.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forest"},{"title":"Fruit","blocks":[{"text":"In botany, a fruit is the seed-bearing structure in flowering plants (angiosperms) that is formed from the ovary after flowering."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Fruit_Platter-_Seasonal_Fruits.jpg/330px-Fruit_Platter-_Seasonal_Fruits.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fruit"},{"title":"Pink","blocks":[{"text":"Pink is a pale tint of red or rose, the color of the pink flower. It was first used as a color name in the late 17th century. A combination of pink and white is associated with innocence, whereas a combination of pink and black links to eroticism and seduction."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Pink_sapphire_ring.jpg/330px-Pink_sapphire_ring.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pink"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Diospyros_melanoxylon_Tendu.jpg/330px-Diospyros_melanoxylon_Tendu.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Diospyros_melanoxylon_Tendu.jpg/330px-Diospyros_melanoxylon_Tendu.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Tendu patta (leaf) collection","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADiospyros_melanoxylon_Tendu.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Adirondacks_2016_Cascade_mountain_hike.jpg/500px-Adirondacks_2016_Cascade_mountain_hike.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Adirondacks_2016_Cascade_mountain_hike.jpg/1280px-Adirondacks_2016_Cascade_mountain_hike.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Adirondacks_2016_Cascade_mountain_hike.jpg/500px-Adirondacks_2016_Cascade_mountain_hike.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The heavily traversed Adirondack Mountains of Upstate New York form the southernmost part of the Eastern forest-boreal transition ecoregion, constituting part of the world's taiga biome.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAdirondacks_2016_Cascade_mountain_hike.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Niepolomice_oli_2013251.jpg/500px-Niepolomice_oli_2013251.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c